-- title:
--   Points of the q-fibre specialise from ξ_∞ or ξ₀
-- statement:
--   Let $N_0$ be a nonzero natural number and $q$ a prime with $q \nmid N_0$, and let $\mathfrak{X}$ be a level-$(N_0,q)$ Deligne–Rapoport package `DRModelPackageLevel N₀ q hqN` for the structure morphism `DRLevel.toBase N₀ q : X N₀ q ⟶ Spec (R q)`. Let $O$ be a commutative local ring together with a ring homomorphism $\rho_O \colon \mathrm{R}\,q \to O$, and assume that the maximal ideal of $O$ is the principal ideal generated by the image of $q$, i.e. $\mathfrak{m}_O = (q)$. Let $\kappa$ be an algebraically closed field of characteristic $q$ and $t \colon O \to \kappa$ a ring homomorphism. Write $\mathfrak{X}_O =$ `DRLevel.XO ρO` for the base change of `X N₀ q` along $\operatorname{Spec}$ of $\rho_O$, with `DRLevel.XO.toBase ρO` its projection to $\operatorname{Spec} O$ (the second pullback projection). Let $y$ be a point of $\mathfrak{X}_O$ which does not lie in the preimage under that projection of the basic open set $D(q) \subseteq \operatorname{Spec} O$; that is, $y$ lies over the locus where $q$ vanishes. Then the two points $\mathfrak{X}.\xi_\infty(\rho_O,t)$ and $\mathfrak{X}.\xi_0(\rho_O,t)$ of $\mathfrak{X}_O$ supplied by the package satisfy: $\xi_\infty$ specialises to $y$, or $\xi_0$ specialises to $y$.
--
--   This is the statement that the $q$-fibre of the base-changed Deligne–Rapoport model is covered by the closures of the two distinguished points $\xi_\infty$ and $\xi_0$ attached to the two components of the mod-$q$ fibre of $X_0(N_0q)$; the reverse inclusion is automatic since the fibre is closed. It is used in the analysis of the crossing points of the fibre, in [`ModularCurve.DRModelPackageLevel.eq_xi_of_ringKrullDim_stalk_le_one`](thm.html#ModularCurve.DRModelPackageLevel.eq_xi_of_ringKrullDim_stalk_le_one), in [`ModularCurve.DRModelPackageLevel.forall_exists_orientedCrossingChart`](thm.html#ModularCurve.DRModelPackageLevel.forall_exists_orientedCrossingChart), and in [`V3AsmLevel.comp_prod`](thm.html#V3AsmLevel.comp_prod).
